Easy to see that  $S_n=S_{n-2}+S_{n-4}+2\times \sum_{i=0}^{n-3}{S_i}$  for all  $n\geqslant 4$  where  $S_0=1$ .
This gives us  $S_{n+1}=S_n+S_{n-1}+S_{n-2}+S_{n-3}-S_{n-4}$  for all  $n\geqslant 4$  and we easily have the following first few values of  $S_n$ :   $$ S_0=1,S_1=0,S_2=1,S_3=2,S_4=4,S_5=8,S_6=15,S_7=28 $$  For each  $n\in \mathbb{Z}^+$ , we denote certain statements as follows:
\begin{align*}
T(n) & : S_{n-1}<S_n
P_1(n) & : \frac{7}{4}S_{n-1}< S_n 
P_2(n) & : S_n< 2 S_{n-1}
Q(n) & : S_n>S_{n-1}+S_{n-2}+S_{n-3}-S_{n-4}
R(n) & : S_{n-1}+S_{n-2}+S_{n-3}-S_{n-4}>\frac{3S_{n}}{4}
\end{align*}
Note that  $T(n-3)\implies T(n+1)$  and  $T(2),T(3),T(4),T(5)$  is true. So  $T(n)$  is true for all  $n\geqslant 2$ .
Easy to see that  $Q(n)\implies P_2(n+1)$  and  $R(n)\implies P_1(n+1)$  for all  $n\in \mathbb{Z}^+$ .

For  $n\geqslant 7$  we have
\begin{align*}
R(n+1)& \iff S_n+S_{n-1}+S_{n-2}+3S_{n-4}>7S_{n-3}
& \iff 2S_{n-1}+2S_{n-2}+4S_{n-4}>6S_{n-3}+S_{n-5}
& \iff 4S_{n-2}+6S_{n-4}+S_{n-5}>4S_{n-3}+2S_{n-6}.
\end{align*}
If  $P_2(n-3)$  is true, combining it with  $4S_{n-2}+S_{n-5}>S_{n-3}+2S_{n-6}$  which follows from  $T(n-5),T(n-3)$  yields the last inequality. Hence we conclude that  $P_2(n-4)\implies R(n)$  for all  $n\geqslant 8$ .

Also, we have 
\begin{align*}
T(n-3) & \implies 2S_{n-3}>S_{n-4}
& \iff S_{n+1}>S_n+S_{n-1}+S_{n-2}-S_{n-3}
& \iff Q(n+1).
\end{align*}

Hence  $Q(n)$  is true for all  $n\geqslant 6\implies P_2(n)$  is true for all  $n\geqslant 7\implies R(n)$  is true for all  $n\geqslant 8\implies P_1(n)$  is true for all  $n\geqslant 9$ . This gives  $\frac{7}{4}S_{n-1}< S_{n}< 2 S_{n-1}$  for all  $n\geqslant 9$ . The rest is just to manually confirm the case  $n\in \{ 7,8\}$  which is easy.

