solution_md:
Well, as stated earlier by Hong Quy, the problem is the same as determining the minimum of  $S:=\sum_{i=1}^n \left(s_i-s_{i+1}ight)^2$ , where  $\left(s_iight)$  is a permutation of  $(1,2,\ldots,n)$  (of course,  $s_{n+1}=s_1$ ).  Now, we write  $s_1$ ,  $s_2$ ,  $\ldots$ ,  $s_n$  on a circle.  Suppose that it takes  $u$  steps from  $1$  to  $n$  in the counterclockwise direction, and  $v$  steps in the other direction.  Obviously,  $u+v=n$ .  As we walk along the counterclockwise direction, define  $a_1$ ,  $a_2$ ,  $\ldots$ ,  $a_u$ , to be the value of  $\left|s_i-s_{i+1}ight|$  observed as we move from  $1$  to  $n$  on the circle.  Define  $b_1$ ,  $b_2$ ,  $\ldots$ ,  $b_v$  similarly when we move in the clockwise direction.

The Triangle Inequality requires that  $\sum_{\mu=1}^u a_\mu \geq n-1$  and  $\sum_{
u=1}^v b_
u \geq n-1$ .  Using the Power-Mean Inequality, we get \[S = \sum_{\mu=1}^u a_\mu^2+\sum_{
u=1}^v b_
u ^2 \geq  rac{(n-1)^2}{u}+ rac{(n-1)^2}{v}\,.\]  Since  $u+v=n$ , the value  $ rac{1}{u}+ rac{1}{v} =  rac{n}{uv}$  is at least  $ rac{4}{n}$  (using AM-GM).  That is, \[S \geq  rac{4}{n}(n-1)^2 = 4n-8+ rac{4}{n}>4n-8\,.\]  As  $S$  need be even, we see that  $S \geq 4n-6 =2^2\cdot(n-2)+1^2\cdot2$ .  It should be trivial to you that there are only two configurations that yield this lower bound.

